  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Wed Aug 12 2026\n\nA subtle shortwave feature is expected to track from the\nNorthern Plains into the Upper Midwest tonight into tomorrow.\nThis will help to provide some synoptic support for storm\nchances. With the stationary boundary expected to remain to our\nsouth, moisture will be one the main questions. Model guidance\nand WPC QPF continue to keep the axis of the highest rain well \nto our south driven by this feature. Even looking at the current\nstate of dew points across Minnesota it is mainly down near the\nborder with Iowa that we still have high dew points with much \nlower values as you head north. However this moisture concern is\nlimited somewhat based on the timing of when storms are \nexpected, overnight. With the diurnal drop in temperature, this\nwill make saturation easier to achieve. Still storm chances, \nespecially when you take where upstream CI is most likely to \noccur remain much higher to our south. Therefore, it is \nsouthern Minnesota with the best chances to see some scattered \nstorms. SPC has maintained a marginal (1 out of 5) risk with a \nhighly conditional risk. Overall trends have been farther south \nin recent CAM runs as the instability environment looks on the\nmore marginal side. Enough for storm storms to form, but deep\nconvection is more uncertain with this level of instability. Some\nruns have shown on favorable deep layer shear, so if deep \nconvection can initiate then a storm could be severe, thus the \nhighly conditional marginal risk. \n\nA more zonal flow period looks to settle in Thursday. This will\nstart a cooling trend through the weekend with temperatures\neventually settling with highs near 80 and lows in the lower \n60s. So while this is a cooling trend it is us falling down to \nnormal temperatures from a warmer than normal period. Rain and \nstorm chances continue to be focused down to our south with some\nchances in regions near the Iowa border. Looking ahead to \nFriday, which had been the next chance for a widespread rain, \nchances have come down. Looking towards both the deterministic \nmodels and their ensemble systems there is much more spread now \nwithin the solutions. The past discussion noted a downward \ntrend and this has continued. Tomorrow this will start to get \nmore into the CAM window and that should provide some useful \nadditional guidance to see if this downward trend continues. \nSome guidance though, most notably the ECMWF, still has this \nchance but it has shifted temporally more into late Saturday \nfrom the morning. So increased uncertainty with both spatial and\ntemporal gaps between ensemble members. With the chances for \nsome later times now during peak heating this does also increase\nthunderstorm chances. We should have a break in activity for \nthe start of the week before a more active period starts again. \nAs expected though there is significant spread for next week.",
